{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he was crushed</b>. From the root of חלל." "<b>And the discipline of our peace</b>. That our peace be continuous, and it is upon him, and the witness is 'and with his stripes we are healed'." "And the meaning of <b>crushed for our transgressions</b> is that HaShem will punish the nations on account of the affliction of Israel, as it is written, 'and I will cleanse their blood, I will not cleanse' (Joel 4:21). And the meaning of 'the discipline of our peace' is known, for all the time that Israel is in the affliction of exile there is peace for the nations — do you not see what is written about the time of salvation, 'and it was a time of distress' (Daniel 12:1)? And furthermore it is written, when the angels returned, 'all the earth sits and is quiet' (Zechariah 1:11), and the angel answered, 'Until when will You not have compassion on Jerusalem' (ibid. 12) — the reason being that all the time there is quiet for the nations, Jerusalem is not shown compassion."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 53, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:59:18.193907985Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}